  "worked_solution": "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\u27f9\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =\\frac{\\sqrt{z}}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061z}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{\u2032}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\sqrt{z}}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061z}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061z}{\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061z}{\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing integration by parts on the left side,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061z & \\,\u27f9\\,{u}^{\u2032}=\\frac{1}{z} \\\\ {v}^{\u2032}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z}} & \\,\u27f9\\,v=2\\sqrt{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061z}{\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 2\\sqrt{z}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061z-2\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 2\\sqrt{z}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061z-4\\sqrt{z} & =x+K \\\\ 2\\sqrt{z}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061z-2) & =x+K \\\\ \\sqrt{z}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061z-2) & =\\frac{x}{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=\\frac{K}{2}.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x+y}\\,(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x+y)-2) & =\\frac{x}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y)=\\sqrt{x+y}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x+y)-2).$",
